# Blobly Net project — J1939 demo (#171), zero drivers, no hardware.
#
# One CAN row, ticked J1939. Nothing here transmits: the demo is the RECORDING beside it.
#
#   BLOBLY_PROJECT=projects/j1939-demo.blobnet ./scripts/run_gui.sh
#   → Trace ▸ Open Recording ▸ samples/j1939-bam.log
#
# and the trace reads what a truck bus actually looks like:
#
#   PGN F004 [00]   and   PGN F004 [21]     one parameter group, two senders
#   PGN EA00 [00>03]                        a request addressed to one node
#   TP.CM BAM [00], TP.DT #1 [00] …   TP     the frames of a multi-packet transfer
#   PGN FEE5 [00] — 3 packets        BAM     the 20-byte message they add up to
#
# Hover the id column for the whole split; type `pgn:fee5` or `sa:21` in the filter box.
#
# The recording carries no database on purpose: this is what the trace can say about a bus
# NOBODY has a DBC for, which is the state a bench is usually in on the first day. Attach one
# and the names replace `PGN ....`.
#
# WHY THE TICK, and why the recording needs none. A live wire has an owner to ask, so it is
# asked: no frame can say it is J1939, since every 29-bit identifier splits into a plausible
# group number and a plausible source address whether or not anybody meant it to. A recording
# has nobody to ask, so it answers for itself — a well-formed transport announcement in the file
# is proof, and nothing else counts.
#
# WHY THERE IS NO REPLAY ROW HERE. Replay puts the recording's frames on the wire, but those
# frames are then OURS, and blobly rebuilds transfers out of what it RECEIVES. That line is
# where it is because a backend decides it: PCAN does not echo our own sends at all, so a
# promise to rebuild them could not be kept on the adapter it would matter most for.
project:
  name: j1939-demo
  version: 2

channels:
  - name: J1939
    type: can
    interface: inproc:J1939   # driver-free; swap for `can0` or `pcan:PCAN_USBBUS1@250000`
    bitrate: 250000           # the J1939 rate; nothing in-process enforces it
    mode: normal
    enabled: true
    j1939: true
